---
import { getFeaturedFaqs } from '../data/faqs';

const faqs = getFeaturedFaqs();

const faqStructuredData = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'FAQPage',
	mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
		'@type': 'Question',
		name: f.q,
		acceptedAnswer: {
			'@type': 'Answer',
			text: f.a,
		},
	})),
};
---

<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas-soft px-4 py-20 sm:px-6">
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
		<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">WHY CINEMATIC PHOTO</span>
		<h2 class="max-w-2xl font-sans text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-4xl">
			A cinematic photo editor built for movie-grade color grading
		</h2>

		<div class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-6 text-base leading-7 text-body">
			<p>
				Cinematic Photo is a free online photo editor that turns an ordinary picture into a
				movie-style still in one click, no cinematic photo editing experience required. Most photo editor apps bury cinematic looks behind
				subscriptions, masking tools, or a steep learning curve. Cinematic Photo works more like a
				colorist's shortcut: pick a preset, nudge a few sliders, export. No tutorial required.
				It's a genuinely easy alternative to Lightroom cinematic presets for anyone who just wants
				the movie aesthetic without installing desktop software or learning curve panels, and a
				faster option than a generic iloveimg photo editor alternative when the look you want is
				specifically cinematic.
			</p>
			<p>
				As a photo editor app, it stays deliberately narrow: instead of trying to be a full
				general-purpose editor with hundreds of unrelated tools, it focuses entirely on one job:
				making a photo look and feel like a frame pulled from a movie. That focus is what makes it
				fast. There's no menu-diving to find the cinematic look; every control on screen exists to
				push a photo closer to a film-grade image, so going from a flat snapshot to a graded still
				takes seconds rather than the many-step workflow a general online photo editor with movie
				filters usually demands.
			</p>

			<h3 class="font-sans text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">
				Movie-inspired color grading presets
			</h3>
			<p>
				Each of the 60 presets in this cinematic editor is modeled on a recognizable film or TV
				look, organized into nine genres: Thriller & Mystery, Crime, Sci-Fi, Drama, War & History,
				Fantasy & Adventure, Action, Superhero, and TV Shows. Reach for the Shawshank Redemption preset for
				a warm, restrained 35mm drama grade, the Pulp Fiction photo filter for pulpy amber-red
				Tarantino tones, or the <a href="/presets/the-dark-knight/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">Dark Knight color grading preset</a> for crushed blacks and a cold
				steel tone. The Inception and <a href="/presets/interstellar/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">Interstellar movie color filter effects</a> deliver Nolan's
				signature cool steel-blue sci-fi palette, the Gladiator preset brings bleached golden
				desert warmth, and the <a href="/presets/the-matrix/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">Matrix green tint photo filter</a> recreates the film's signature
				high-gain digital green. Every option applies a cinematic filter to a photo for free,
				instantly, directly on the canvas — browse the{' '}
				<a href="/presets/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">full list of all 60 presets</a>{' '}
				to find the exact film your photo needs.
			</p>

			<h3 class="font-sans text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">
				Film texture and cinematic framing
			</h3>
			<p>
				Beyond color, Cinematic Photo adds the texture and framing details that sell a movie
				look. Add film grain to a photo online for free with a single slider, dial in a
				vignette, or add glow and halation the way anamorphic lenses bloom around bright light
				sources, the closest thing to <a href="/guides/cinematic-color-grading/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">cinematic color grading</a> with
				a movie LUT, without dedicated grading software. Add a cinematic letterbox to a photo online
				with one toggle to crop to a 2.39:1 cinematic aspect ratio, complete with black bars
				across the top and bottom of the frame, and stamp a film-stock label in the corner for a
				finish inspired by the <a href="/looks/vintage-35mm-film/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">vintage 35mm film look</a>.
				For a full walkthrough, see how to <a href="/guides/how-to-make-photos-look-cinematic/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">make any photo look cinematic</a>.
			</p>

			<h3 class="font-sans text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">
				Private by design: nothing ever leaves your device
			</h3>
			<p>
				This is a browser based cinematic photo editor with client side photo editor
				architecture from the ground up: no uploads, no accounts, and no server ever sees your
				image. Every filter, grade, and export renders locally on an HTML canvas in your
				browser tab, so a quick movie look photo filter is available even on a slow connection,
				and your photos stay yours.
			</p>

			<h3 class="font-sans text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">
				Who it's for
			</h3>
			<p>
				Photographers, filmmakers, and social creators use Cinematic Photo as a free online
				photo aesthetic filter tool for thumbnails, posters, and portfolio stills. Think of it
				as an indie film photo effect generator for anyone building a Hollywood style photo
				filter into their workflow: an online movie color grader that turns a flat photo into a
				film-grade image in the time it takes to drag a slider. See curated preset picks for{' '}
				<a href="/for/instagram/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">Instagram</a>,{' '}
				<a href="/for/youtube-thumbnails/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">YouTube thumbnails</a>,{' '}
				<a href="/for/travel-photography/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">travel photography</a>, and{' '}
				<a href="/for/portraits/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">cinematic portraits</a>,
				or see how it compares to{' '}
				<a href="/alternatives/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">Lightroom presets, VSCO, and other cinematic editing tools</a>.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-16 border-t border-hairline pt-10">
			<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">FAQ</span>
			<div class="flex flex-col divide-y divide-hairline">
				{
					faqs.map((faq) => (
						<details class="group py-5">
							<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-sans text-base font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
								<span>{faq.q}</span>
								<svg
									class="h-4 w-4 flex-shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180"
									viewBox="0 0 24 24"
									fill="none"
									stroke="currentColor"
									stroke-width="2"
									stroke-linecap="round"
									stroke-linejoin="round"
								>
									<polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9" />
								</svg>
							</summary>
							<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-body">{faq.a}</p>
						</details>
					))
				}
			</div>
			<a
				href="/faq/"
				class="mt-6 inline-block font-sans text-sm font-medium text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink"
			>
				See all frequently asked questions →
			</a>
		</div>
	</div>

	<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqStructuredData)} />
</section>
